    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1007">

                <pb facs="1063" n="1053" sameAs="frus:frus1949v09/pg1053"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">661.9331/4–1549: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister-Counselor of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Clark</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Canton</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-04-15T12:00:00+08:00">April 15,
                            1949—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received April 16—1:44 a. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Cantel 219. George Yeh says he accepted position Acting Foreign Minister only
                    after assurances from Li Tsung-jen and Ho Ying-chin<anchor n="30"
                        xml:id="d1007-f1"/> that effort Chang Chih-chung to speed up negotiations
                    Sino-Soviet trade pact be resisted and no further action taken thereon so long
                    as he was Acting Foreign Minister. Yeh said Chang Chih-chung was proposing
                    accord Soviets mineral rights in contravention Chinese law and interests and
                    under condition which in view China’s treaty <pb facs="1064" n="1054"
                        xml:id="pg1054"/> obligations might prove embarrassing. Accordingly,
                    Sino-Soviet trade negotiations had stopped for time being.</p>
                <p>Sent Department, repeated Nanking 193, Shanghai 141.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Clark</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1007-f1" n="30" xml:id="d1007-f1-content">
                <p>General Ho succeeded Sun Fo as President of the Chinese Executive Yuan in March,
                    1949.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
